## Approach
Iterate from 1 to n. For each number, check divisibility by 15 first (both 3 and 5), then by 3, then by 5, otherwise print the number itself. Checking 15 first avoids printing "Fizz" or "Buzz" alone on multiples of both.

## Code
```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"strconv"
	"strings"
)

func fizzBuzz(n int) []string {
	result := make([]string, 0, n)
	for i := 1; i <= n; i++ {
		switch {
		case i%15 == 0:
			result = append(result, "FizzBuzz")
		case i%3 == 0:
			result = append(result, "Fizz")
		case i%5 == 0:
			result = append(result, "Buzz")
		default:
			result = append(result, strconv.Itoa(i))
		}
	}
	return result
}

func main() {
	fmt.Println(strings.Join(fizzBuzz(15), " "))
}
```

## Complexity
Time is O(n), one pass over the range. Space is O(n) for the result slice, or O(1) extra if you print instead of collecting.

