Here's the fix with `let`:

```js
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 100);
}
```

That prints 0, 1, 2, because `let` creates a new binding for `i` on each iteration, so each callback closes over its own copy.

If you have to keep `var`, capture the value in an IIFE:

```js
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  (function (j) {
    setTimeout(() => console.log(j), 100);
  })(i);
}
```

